      "Continuation of the same scheduled routine on claude/upbeat-dijkstra-tg1n4z. Mid-session, while finishing a separate observability scaffold, origin/main advanced with PR #898 (observability-error-tracking-scaffold) — a sibling session had independently built the exact same feature (dormant vendor-agnostic error-reporting webhook wired into the Stripe webhook's outer catch), more completely than this session's own in-progress version (field-length soft-caps mirroring the repo's established convention, injectable EnvLike for testability, a launch-checklist doc entry). Per AGENT_PROTOCOL's dual-build-collision rule ('if neither side has pushed feature commits yet, the earlier remote claim commit owns it and the later agent pivots'), and since this session had not yet pushed anything, discarded its own local duplicate entirely (git checkout -- on every touched file, rm -rf the new directory) and reset the branch cleanly to the new origin/main tip rather than shipping a competing second implementation.",
    rationale:
      "Rather than re-run a fourth consecutive tiers-1-4 sweep (three in a row this session alone came back clean, most recently via independent line-by-line re-verification), dispatched a research agent to audit the brand-new PR #898 code specifically — a genuinely fresh target never previously reviewed by anyone, distinct from re-checking already-many-times-audited code. It found buildErrorTrackingPayload (src/lib/observability/error-tracking.ts) caps every other field — MAX_CONTEXT_VALUE_LENGTH (500) on each context value, MAX_STACK_LENGTH (2000) on the stack trace — but left `message` (derived from error.message or String(error) for a non-Error thrown value) completely unbounded before JSON.stringify-ing it into the POST body. Independently re-verified by reading buildErrorTrackingPayload directly (line 113 had zero truncation logic, unlike the stack field three lines below it) and cross-checking error-tracking.test.ts's existing coverage (truncation tests exist for context values, none for message).\n\nConcrete scenario: the webhook's outer catch (shipped in the same PR) calls captureError for exactly the class of genuinely-unexpected error this scaffold exists to catch. If that unexpected error is a non-Error thrown value that stringifies to a large blob, or an Error whose .message embeds a large payload (a raw string throw, a JSON-parse failure echoing the offending body, a dependency that stuffs a full response into its message), the size bound this module otherwise consistently enforces silently doesn't apply to the one field most likely to actually be large. Impact is bounded (the send itself still has its own 4-second AbortController timeout, so this can't hang the webhook response) but real: an oversized POST body to whatever endpoint the operator configured, a bandwidth/ingest-quota concern on their Datadog/Sentry-proxy/custom receiver.\n\nFixed by adding MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH (500, matching MAX_CONTEXT_VALUE_LENGTH) and truncating message the same way stack already was — a rawMessage intermediate is computed first, then capped with the identical ternary-plus-ellipsis pattern the stack field already used, keeping the fix local and consistent with the file's existing style rather than introducing a new shared helper for a two-line change. Added 3 new Vitest cases to error-tracking.test.ts: an oversized Error.message truncates, an oversized non-Error thrown value truncates the same way, and a message at or under the cap is left untouched (proving this isn't an unconditional truncate that would corrupt short, normal messages). Verified the new tests actually catch the regression: git stash'd the source change alone (test file kept) and confirmed both new truncation tests failed with the full 1000-character untruncated message before restoring the fix.\n\nAdded one paragraph to /docs/architecture directly after the observability-error-tracking-scaffold writeup naming the gap and the fix, in place. src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts — this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx — the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 36ea829c (this session's own starting tip after the pivot, post-PR-#898).\n\nFile-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: prompt_process.tsx 228,067 bytes, business-decisions.ts 209,277 bytes at session start (both via wc -c) — both comfortably under the 262,144-byte trigger, no re-archive needed this round.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (error-tracking.test.ts, 19/19 passed) then full suite (3593 passed, 211 files — 3 net new) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings) + npm run dev smoke check curling /dashboard/leads (200 immediately) and /docs/architecture (200 after its own well-documented slow first-Turbopack-compile, confirmed the new paragraph renders). Killed the dev server, removed the stray gitignored local.db/local.db-shm/local.db-wal it created, and confirmed git status showed only the intended five files changed before this commit (this iteration is a pure logic + docs fix — no new file, no route/UI surface change beyond the docs paragraph).",
