    rationale:
      "Rather than a fourth consecutive tiers-1-4 sweep, dispatched a research agent explicitly briefed on everything closed today (the race-guard fix, three clean tiers-1-4 sweeps, the observability scaffold, and its message-length-cap fix) and pointed at genuinely fresh angles instead. It identified that the observability-error-tracking-scaffold entry itself explicitly named two future candidate failure paths — 'a Convex-outage SQLite-fallback trigger' and 'a Resend delivery failure' — and that neither had been touched by any later entry today. It ruled out the Convex-fallback candidate as too large for one iteration (confirmed via grep: 84 separate hand-written console.warn 'falling back to SQLite' sites across ~80 exported functions in src/lib/db.ts, no shared wrapper to hook into) and recommended the Resend candidate instead: src/lib/email/resend.ts's two Resend HTTP-call catch blocks (sendEmail and sendOperatorDailyDigest — confirmed via grep these are the only two fetch(\"https://api.resend.com/emails\") call sites in the file, with every other send helper funneling through sendEmail) console.error'd and returned { success: false, error } without ever calling captureError. Independently re-verified by reading both catch blocks directly and confirming neither file imported observability/error-tracking before this fix.\n\nWired captureError into both: sendEmail's catch now calls `await captureError(error, { component: \"resend-email\", leadSlug })` — leadSlug (the internal lead identifier), not the recipient email address, is forwarded, since an operator-configured ERROR_TRACKING_WEBHOOK_URL may not be a vetted PII processor and almost every sendEmail caller sends to a real business contact's address, not the operator's own. sendOperatorDailyDigest's catch calls `await captureError(error, { component: \"resend-operator-digest\" })` (no recipient forwarded either, kept parallel). Both mirror the Stripe webhook's existing best-effort, non-blocking contract exactly — a capture failure can never mask or replace the original send failure the catch already handles, since captureError never throws.\n\nAdded 3 new Vitest cases to resend.test.ts (a new describe block, using vi.doMock to swap in a spy on captureError per-test since the module-level vi.mock convention this file otherwise uses for @/lib/db doesn't fit a per-test assertion on call arguments): sendEmail calls captureError with the correct component + leadSlug on a non-2xx Resend response, sendEmail does NOT call captureError on a successful send, and sendOperatorDailyDigest calls captureError with its own component on a thrown network error. Verified the tests actually catch the regression: git stash'd resend.ts's source change alone (test file kept) and confirmed both sendEmail/sendOperatorDailyDigest capture-assertion tests failed (captureErrorMock called 0 times) with the exact pre-fix behavior before restoring the fix.\n\nAdded one paragraph to /docs/architecture directly after the error-tracking-message-length-cap writeup naming the two originally-named candidates, which one this closes, and why the other (Convex-fallback) stays explicitly out of scope pending a dedicated future iteration (either touching all 84 call sites or first extracting a shared wrapper — real regression risk in the most heavily-tested file in the repo, not a same-session addition). src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts — this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx — the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to a3a604e8 (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#899).\n\nFile-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: prompt_process.tsx 232,174 bytes, business-decisions.ts 214,876 bytes at session start (both via wc -c) — both comfortably under the 262,144-byte trigger, no re-archive needed this round.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (resend.test.ts, 75/75 passed) then full suite (3596 passed, 211 files — 3 net new) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). This is a server-only lib fix with no route/UI surface change beyond a docs paragraph, so no dev-server smoke check was needed beyond the automated build/test/lint gate.",
