    rationale:
      "Dispatched a research agent explicitly briefed on all five things closed today (the race-guard fix, three clean tiers-1-4 sweeps, the observability scaffold, its message-length-cap fix, and the Resend capture-error wiring) and told the one remaining named observability candidate (a Convex-outage SQLite-fallback trigger in db.ts) was already investigated and ruled out as too large (84 scattered console.warn sites, no shared choke point) — asked to find something genuinely fresh instead, or a narrower slice of the db.ts gap if one existed. It found a structural blind spot in the webhook's own outer try/catch that none of today's three prior observability entries had noticed: that outer catch (added in the same PR as the scaffold itself) only fires for an exception that escapes every inner catch — but nearly every one of this file's ~40 webhookFulfillmentFailureResponse(...) sites returns its own 5xx JSON response from inside its catch block rather than re-throwing. A return inside a catch is ordinary control flow, not a thrown exception, so it structurally can never reach the outer catch's captureError call. Independently re-verified by reading the JS semantics directly (not trusting the claim) and confirming no other capture path exists for these sites — including the single highest-stakes one: the catch wrapping recordLeadPayment + enqueueProductionHandoff for bundle checkouts (checkout.session.completed / async_payment_succeeded), the actual payment-ledger write.\n\nConcrete scenario: if recordLeadPayment or enqueueProductionHandoff throws (a Convex/SQLite failure, a schema mismatch, any bug), the existing catch already correctly returns 5xx so Stripe retries — the money-safety contract AGENT_PROTOCOL documents was never at risk — but the failure was invisible to the error-tracking scaffold shipped earlier today, meaning the one case that most directly means 'a paying customer's payment isn't recorded and their site handoff didn't happen' had zero first-class observability despite the scaffold's own stated purpose. Rather than sweep all ~40 sites (the identical regression-risk reasoning already applied to db.ts's 84 sites in the prior entry, in this same file, the most heavily-tested one in the repo), wired captureError into just this one — the highest-value single site, confirmed via the same 'which failure most needs first-class capture' survey question the original scaffold's own decision entry posed.\n\nShipped: src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts — added `await captureError(dbError, { component: \"stripe-webhook\", eventType: event.type, eventId: event.id, failureKind: \"bundle_payment_or_handoff\" })` inside the existing catch, before its existing console.error/return, with a comment explaining why this site needed direct wiring rather than relying on the outer catch. src/app/api/stripe/webhook/route.test.ts — extended the existing 'returns 500 (bundle_payment_or_handoff) so Stripe retries when recordLeadPayment fails' test (rather than adding a parallel duplicate) with an assertion that captureError was called with the exact error object and the correct context shape. Verified the test actually catches the regression: git stash'd route.ts's source change alone (test file kept) and confirmed the extended assertion failed (0 calls) with the exact pre-fix behavior before restoring the fix.\n\nAdded one paragraph to /docs/architecture directly after the resend-email-capture-error-wiring writeup naming the structural gap, why it evaded the outer catch, the fix, and explicitly flagging the other ~39 unwired sites in this file as a real but deliberately deferred gap, not a full sweep. src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts — this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx — the accompanying ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change — all five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked, unchanged. Diff-gate anchor moves to 20d1184f (this session's own starting tip, post-PR-#900).\n\nFile-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: prompt_process.tsx 237,031 bytes, business-decisions.ts 220,025 bytes at session start (both via wc -c) — both comfortably under the 262,144-byte trigger, no re-archive needed this round.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (route.test.ts, 229/229 passed) then full suite (3596 passed, 211 files, unchanged count — an existing test was extended, not duplicated) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). This is a server-only orchestration fix with no route/UI surface change beyond a docs paragraph, so no dev-server smoke check was needed beyond the automated build/test/lint gate.",
