    decision:
      "Continuation of the same scheduled routine on claude/upbeat-dijkstra-tg1n4z, picked up at origin/main tip 9736d9c9 (PR #909, buffer-schedule-stale-pack-variant-guard, merged this session). Confirmed baseline green: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean). A git fetch --prune sweep and mcp__github__list_pull_requests found no live sibling branch and no direct file collision; the sole other open PR remains #202 (unrelated, weeks-stale human draft).",
    rationale:
      "A dispatched research agent, told the observability/captureError axis and the Buffer-scheduling stale-content-guard family were both now thoroughly covered and not to re-propose either without a genuinely different failure mode, was explicitly pointed at checking for other same-shaped 'staleness checked once before a loop, not once per iteration' bugs elsewhere in the dashboard actions file, since buffer-schedule-stale-pack-variant-guard had just proven that exact shape is a real, previously-missed pattern worth hunting for deliberately rather than assuming it was a one-off. It found the identical shape in a structurally different subsystem: archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction (src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts) already re-reads the Commerce catalog once, right before archiving, to drop any orphan whose skuId reappeared via a concurrent saveCommerceCatalogAction (orphan-archive-lost-update-guard) — but archiveCommerceStripeProducts (src/lib/commerce/stripe-sync.ts) then archives its caller-supplied orphan list in a plain sequential for-loop, one awaited stripe.products.update call per item, with no re-check between iterations. If an operator has multiple orphans to clean up and a concurrent catalog save re-adds one of those SKU ids while an earlier item in the loop is still being archived, the reappeared SKU's Product gets deactivated on its own turn anyway — the pre-loop check only protected against a reappearance before the loop started, not one landing mid-loop. Confirmed directly by reading both files and archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts's existing race test, which only covers the before-the-loop case (a single orphan, checked once).\n\nWhy it matters: Stripe rejects new Checkout Session creation for a Price whose Product has been archived, so a customer clicking Buy now on the SKU that reappeared right after this race gets a hard Stripe error with zero operator-visible signal it was self-inflicted — it looks like a random Stripe failure. Self-heals only once the operator happens to re-run Sync to Stripe (findProductByKey reactivates a matched-but-inactive Product on resync), so the window is real but bounded and easy to miss in the interim — the same severity/shape tier as the Buffer-scheduling bug just closed, just in the Commerce catalog cleanup path instead of marketing content delivery.\n\nFixed by giving archiveCommerceStripeProducts an optional isStillOrphan callback, invoked before EACH individual stripe.products.update call rather than checked once outside the function. Kept archiveCommerceStripeProducts's own convenience-wrapper caller (archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProducts, used by tests/scripts with no concurrent-edit window per its own doc comment) unaffected by leaving the option optional and defaulting to the prior always-archive behavior when omitted. archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction now passes the full, unfiltered candidate list plus an isStillOrphan callback that re-reads getCommerceCatalog and checks the fresh skuId set per candidate, instead of pre-filtering once into a toArchive array before calling the archive function — mirroring the per-iteration-not-just-once-per-loop fix pattern buffer-schedule-stale-pack-variant-guard just established, applied here to a different call site with a different resource (Stripe Products, not Buffer posts) and a different concurrent writer (saveCommerceCatalogAction, not generateMarketingPackAction). MAX_COMMERCE_SKUS caps a catalog at 50 items, so the extra per-item getCommerceCatalog reads are affordable for this operator-triggered, low-frequency cleanup action — no batching/caching complexity added.\n\nShipped: src/lib/commerce/stripe-sync.ts - archiveCommerceStripeProducts's signature gained an optional third `options` parameter with an `isStillOrphan` async predicate, checked before each stripe.products.update call inside the existing for-loop; orphans for which it resolves false are skipped (not archived) rather than throwing or aborting the whole batch. src/app/dashboard/leads/actions.ts - archiveOrphanCommerceStripeProductsAction no longer does its own one-time freshCatalog/freshSkuIds/toArchive computation; instead passes the full candidates list plus an isStillOrphan callback wired to a fresh getCommerceCatalog(slug) read per candidate. src/lib/commerce/stripe-sync.test.ts - added archiveCommerceStripeProducts to the test file's imports (previously only its convenience wrapper was directly tested) and three new tests against the real function with a fake Stripe client: unchanged default behavior with no isStillOrphan option, skipping a single orphan the callback reports as no-longer-orphaned, and — the core regression case — three orphans where the callback is invoked once per orphan in order (proven via a checkedSkuIds accumulator) and a reappearance detected on the SECOND orphan only skips that one, leaving its neighbors archived. src/app/dashboard/leads/archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts - updated the existing 'archives orphans...' test's call-shape assertion for the new three-argument signature, and replaced the old 're-reads the catalog and drops a reappeared candidate' test (whose drop logic now lives inside the mocked-away archiveCommerceStripeProducts, so it could no longer observe that behavior at this boundary) with two tests that capture the action's isStillOrphan callback directly from the mock's call args and invoke it manually against both a reappeared and a still-orphaned catalog state, preserving equivalent behavioral coverage of the action's own wiring. Verified all five new/changed assertions actually catch the regression: git stash'd both stripe-sync.ts's and actions.ts's source changes together and confirmed all five failed (two stripe-sync.test.ts assertions with the wrong archived/checked lists, three archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts assertions on the old two-argument call shape or a callback that no longer exists) before restoring the fix. Added one paragraph to /docs/architecture directly after buffer-schedule-stale-pack-variant-guard's writeup, naming the shared bug shape explicitly. src/lib/process/business-decisions.ts - this entry. src/lib/process/prompt_process.tsx - a new NEXT_STEPS done entry plus this ITERATIONS entry. No NEXT_STEPS change - the five gated items (stripe-price-objects, lead-ingestion, marketing-content-scheduling, commerce-payouts-stripe-connect, github-actions-runner-unavailable) remain operator/legal/infra-blocked. Diff-gate anchor moves to 9736d9c9 (origin/main tip after PR #909 merged, this session's own starting point for this iteration).\n\nFile-size check per AGENT_PROTOCOL's re-archive note: both prompt_process.tsx (~203KB) and business-decisions.ts (~128KB) measured well under the 262,144-byte trigger before this entry - no re-archive needed this round.\n\nVerified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npx vitest run targeted (stripe-sync.test.ts 24/24, archive-orphan-commerce-stripe-products-action.test.ts 12/12) then full suite (3611 passed, 212 files - 4 net new) + a full clean rebuild (rm -rf .next && npm run build, clean, same route split, no new warnings). No dev-server smoke check needed beyond the automated build/test/lint gate - server-action-only change with no new route/UI surface, requiring a live STRIPE_SECRET_KEY and a real Commerce catalog neither meaningfully exercisable in this sandboxed environment beyond the existing automated test coverage.",
